WEBVTT

NOTE Silicon Valley C Players, Chapter 17b: Building Blocks — captions generated from the narration script

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.890
Silicon Valley C Players. Chapter seventeen B.

2
00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:10.080
Building Blocks. How a result I never set as a goal came out of stacked foundations.

3
00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:12.326
In twenty eighteen I was a C.T.O.

4
00:00:12.326 --> 00:00:19.908
at a SaaS company, spending my time building strong platforms for other people, and I realized I had built no foundation for myself.

5
00:00:19.908 --> 00:00:22.033
I was not feeling good about my body.

6
00:00:22.033 --> 00:00:24.389
I had gained weight without realizing it.

7
00:00:24.389 --> 00:00:25.480
I was always tired.

8
00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.265
This chapter is that story, in my own voice.

9
00:00:28.265 --> 00:00:31.462
It is not a fiction chapter, and it is not Charlie Hack's.

10
00:00:31.462 --> 00:00:37.250
The times and places below are from official race results, or from what I have already put on the record.

11
00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:42.821
In a Dale Carnegie course, a vision-board exercise produced a goal I had not planned: a triathlon.

12
00:00:42.821 --> 00:00:54.243
The board also included learning guitar — I bought a guitar and learned a few chords, but that has not gone anywhere yet — and starting to play piano again, which I had learned the fundamentals of when I was young.

13
00:00:54.243 --> 00:01:00.328
It talked about some career things, like retiring by the time I was fifty, which I missed, but maybe not for long.

14
00:01:00.328 --> 00:01:02.410
And then some business-specific things.

15
00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.468
I did not make a perfect plan.

16
00:01:04.468 --> 00:01:06.517
I learned what the next step needed.

17
00:01:06.517 --> 00:01:11.128
In twenty nineteen I joined Orangetheory Fitness and started running and lifting.

18
00:01:11.128 --> 00:01:14.658
Within six months I moved to CrossFit, at CrossFit Nine in St.

19
00:01:14.658 --> 00:01:20.635
Petersburg, Florida, the gym I trained at from at least October twenty twenty through twenty twenty-four.

20
00:01:20.635 --> 00:01:24.791
In twenty twenty-two I earned a CrossFit Level One trainer certification.

21
00:01:24.791 --> 00:01:27.580
I studied the methodology, including its critics.

22
00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:30.648
That gym is where the method started to click.

23
00:01:30.648 --> 00:01:35.240
CrossFit is largely gymnastics-focused, and gymnastics is bodyweight movement.

24
00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:39.184
Skill and positions come first, then the work you can do with them.

25
00:01:39.184 --> 00:01:41.480
Weightlifting is part of that work too.

26
00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:47.950
The order I kept coming back to is mechanics first, then consistency, and only then intensity.

27
00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:51.726
I was after virtuosity: years of practice, not a shortcut.

28
00:01:51.726 --> 00:01:59.930
That sequence is the method I actually used: not a complete program written in advance, but the next block, then the next one.

29
00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:03.502
This is the same way I have tried to work in my career.

30
00:02:03.502 --> 00:02:07.192
I build platforms and models, not products or specific numbers.

31
00:02:07.192 --> 00:02:14.045
CrossFit is an abstraction in that sense: functional fitness as a model of health, rather than a chase for one score.

32
00:02:14.045 --> 00:02:19.610
Weak foundations in life, in tech, in marketing, or in finance mean nothing lasting gets built.

33
00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:24.534
The surprise later in this chapter is an example of that, not a contradiction of it.

34
00:02:24.534 --> 00:02:29.488
A time I never set as a goal, when I started, came out of the blocks I had already stacked.

35
00:02:29.488 --> 00:02:31.666
A ring muscle-up is one of those blocks.

36
00:02:31.666 --> 00:02:34.008
I spent years on it. I worked with a coach.

37
00:02:34.008 --> 00:02:36.240
We built the fundamental strengths first.

38
00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:39.129
Skill and technique override power and strength.

39
00:02:39.129 --> 00:02:45.077
The false grip is a gymnastics technique most people don't know about, and it is part of how the movement works.

40
00:02:45.077 --> 00:02:48.900
You don't muscle your way through the rings if the position isn't there.

41
00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:53.650
That is a building block, the same as a platform at work: you stand on it later.

42
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.216
Double unders are the same kind of block.

43
00:02:56.216 --> 00:02:59.620
You can't do them until you can do a long set of single unders.

44
00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:03.185
I finally got them, but they only worked when I was fresh.

45
00:03:03.185 --> 00:03:10.780
In the middle of an extreme workout, under fatigue, they fell apart, because I had never tested them under load or under heavy breathing.

46
00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.998
The next block was endurance on top of the skill.

47
00:03:13.998 --> 00:03:18.389
That is what gets you to unbroken double unders when you are already tired.

48
00:03:18.389 --> 00:03:23.189
Today I can do fifty, seventy-five, or a hundred double unders in a row, unbroken.

49
00:03:23.189 --> 00:03:24.360
That took six years.

50
00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:34.525
Mechanics first, then consistency, then intensity: a long set of singles, then doubles that hold up fresh, then doubles that hold up when you are breathing hard.

51
00:03:34.525 --> 00:03:36.720
The result was never the first step.

52
00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:40.046
Running was a ladder too. A mile seemed hard.

53
00:03:40.046 --> 00:03:43.563
Then the miles got faster. Then five Ks, then ten Ks.

54
00:03:43.563 --> 00:03:46.217
Everyday runs became a minimum of ten K.

55
00:03:46.217 --> 00:03:49.070
Then I was building toward a half marathon.

56
00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:51.986
Finishing a half is one kind of endurance.

57
00:03:51.986 --> 00:03:53.697
Excelling at one is another.

58
00:03:53.697 --> 00:04:07.078
At the Carlsbad Half Marathon, on January fourteenth, twenty twenty-four, I ran one twenty-nine thirty-four official, eighty-ninth of three thousand three hundred ninety-five, and fourth in men forty-five to forty-nine.

59
00:04:07.078 --> 00:04:10.071
That is sub-three-hour marathon pace, for a half.

60
00:04:10.071 --> 00:04:16.670
It came out of the same stack: the mile that had seemed hard, then the everyday ten K, then the longer work.

61
00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:19.049
Swimming had its own first block.

62
00:04:19.049 --> 00:04:21.755
I was fearful of a couple of laps in a pool.

63
00:04:21.755 --> 00:04:25.690
I got to swimming one point two miles in open water, with waves.

64
00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:29.107
The bike started as leisurely rides of a few miles.

65
00:04:29.107 --> 00:04:32.956
Then thirty-mile and forty-mile rides became an average Tuesday.

66
00:04:32.956 --> 00:04:38.670
Then, in a half triathlon, I rode fifty-six miles at a race pace of twenty-plus miles per hour.

67
00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:42.722
I did half triathlons. Then I stacked further: a full triathlon.

68
00:04:42.722 --> 00:04:49.200
A two point four mile swim, a one hundred twelve mile bike, and then a twenty-six point two mile run after that.

69
00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.340
Stacking, and stacking, and stacking.

70
00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:58.312
My first marathon was the Clearwater Running Festival, in Clearwater Beach, on April twenty-fifth, twenty twenty-one.

71
00:04:58.312 --> 00:05:02.620
I signed up a week before, without a full marathon-training block behind me.

72
00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:05.398
The official time was five fifty-six twenty-four.

73
00:05:05.398 --> 00:05:11.010
I finished: one hundred twenty-fourth overall, and eighth of eight in men forty-five to forty-nine.

74
00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:14.073
I had thought fitness alone might be enough.

75
00:05:14.073 --> 00:05:17.464
It was not, not for that distance, not that first time.

76
00:05:17.464 --> 00:05:25.357
A second marathon came after more structured training: the REVEL Big Cottonwood Marathon, on September tenth, twenty twenty-two.

77
00:05:25.357 --> 00:05:28.316
Official chip time three twenty-five forty-five.

78
00:05:28.316 --> 00:05:33.064
The drop from nearly six hours to three twenty-five was not a secret workout.

79
00:05:33.064 --> 00:05:37.380
It was a foundation I had not built the first time: a structured plan.

80
00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:47.090
On April first, twenty twenty-three, at forty-seven, I ran the REVEL Mount Charleston Marathon in three oh three fifty-five chip, a seven-oh-one pace.

81
00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:52.394
I had never imagined a three-oh-three, and I had not set it as a goal when I started.

82
00:05:52.394 --> 00:05:59.881
It emerged from the building blocks: Orangetheory, CrossFit, the swim, the bike, the run, and the races that came after.

83
00:05:59.881 --> 00:06:03.750
That time was also my Boston qualifier for twenty twenty-four.

84
00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:07.950
A personal best like that is easy to misread as the point of the work.

85
00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:10.920
It was a result. The work was the stack underneath it.

86
00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:19.372
On February tenth, twenty twenty-four, at the Mesa Marathon, I was chasing a sub-three that day and finished in three eighteen forty-three.

87
00:06:19.372 --> 00:06:25.718
On April fifteenth, twenty twenty-four, I ran the Boston Marathon in three forty-three twenty-seven official.

88
00:06:25.718 --> 00:06:30.026
It was, as I wrote at the time, not my fastest but definitely the funnest.

89
00:06:30.026 --> 00:06:35.790
As I wrote after the race, I just got close to the wall and ran parallel with it for sixteen miles.

90
00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:39.688
After Boston I paused that stretch of marathon training.

91
00:06:39.688 --> 00:06:48.747
I kept going in other ways. In May twenty twenty-four I ran the Dunedin Hog Hustle five K in twenty twenty-three, third male and first masters.

92
00:06:48.747 --> 00:06:54.258
In January twenty twenty-five I ran the Carlsbad Marathon in three forty-four oh seven.

93
00:06:54.258 --> 00:07:00.340
In February twenty twenty-five I ran the Publix Gasparilla fifteen K in one oh five twenty-nine.

94
00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:08.178
I competed in the CrossFit Open from twenty twenty-one through twenty twenty-four, in men forty-five to forty-nine, at CrossFit Nine.

95
00:07:08.178 --> 00:07:10.095
I started where a C player starts.

96
00:07:10.095 --> 00:07:20.920
In the twenty twenty-one Open I reached wall walks at the cap in twenty-one point one, and in twenty-one point three and twenty-one point four I got to the bar muscle-ups and could not do any.

97
00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:26.911
I qualified for the Age-Group Quarterfinals in twenty twenty-four, but I did not compete officially.

98
00:07:26.911 --> 00:07:30.578
I did the workouts unofficially, and I scaled them, after Boston.

99
00:07:30.578 --> 00:07:33.003
I still call myself a C player in CrossFit.

100
00:07:33.003 --> 00:07:37.685
The point is the same one as the rest of this book: I was not the best in the room.

101
00:07:37.685 --> 00:07:40.280
I kept stacking foundations, and I kept going.

102
00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:49.000
On December seventh, twenty twenty-five, for my fiftieth birthday, I did a fifty-rep chipper bookended by a five K run and a five K row.

103
00:07:49.340 --> 00:08:00.512
Chapter thirty-six, Sick, Well, Fit, takes this idea into how a company should use AI The order is the same: fundamentals first, then consistency, and only then intensity.

104
00:08:00.512 --> 00:08:03.517
AI multiplies the foundation you already have.

105
00:08:03.517 --> 00:08:05.020
It does not replace it.

106
00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:11.810
The rule I take from this path is simple enough to follow on a Monday, and simple enough to hand to an agent.
